/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  thermoPhysPropDict (v2) -- the donepezil tablet plant.

  THE FLUIDS.  Water (the granulation liquid, the coating suspension's
  carrier and the humidity of the drying air), nitrogen and oxygen (the
  air).  An IDEAL liquid (Raoult) and an IDEAL gas: the only vapour-liquid
  question this plant asks is how much water the drying air carries away,
  and for water into air at one atmosphere that is the right first answer.
  There is NO ORGANIC SOLVENT in this plant (wet granulation with an
  aqueous binder, an aqueous film coat), so no solvent record, no solvent
  recovery and no hazardous-area question of a solvent vapour.

  THE SOLIDS.  The API and every excipient are `role nonvolatile;`
  case-local records in constant/components/: they never enter the vapour,
  they carry no vapour pressure and no critical constants, and every stream
  carries them in its SOLID phase.  NOTHING DISSOLVES in this model: the
  hydroxypropyl cellulose is carried as a solid through the binder tank, and
  the hypromellose and the macrogol through the coating-suspension tank.
  The heat of solution is therefore neglected, and the README says so.

  No transport block: no unit of this plant reads a viscosity, a
  conductivity or a diffusivity (the two dryers dry to their sorption
  equilibrium; there is no spray dryer).
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

recordType    thermophysicalPropertySystem;
schemaVersion 2;

components
(
    water  N2  O2
    donepezilHydrochloride
    lactoseMonohydrate  maizeStarch  microcrystallineCellulose
    hydroxypropylCellulose  magnesiumStearate
    hypromellose  polyethyleneGlycol  talcPowder  titaniumDioxide  yellowIronOxide
);

equilibrium
{
    formulation gammaPhi;

    liquid
    {
        activityModel ideal;            // gamma = 1 (Raoult)
        standardState pureLiquid;
    }

    vapour
    {
        fugacityModel idealGas;
    }
}

